{
const id='dp-base-cases',x=item(id);
insert(id,2,'    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError("n must be non-negative")',['Reject a negative factorial argument before applying the recurrence.','Raise ValueError: factorial is defined here only for non-negative integers.']);
x.explanation='A recursive function calls itself while making progress toward a base case it can answer directly. Progress must be well-founded: it may shrink an integer, shorten a remaining suffix, or finish another position. A base case alone is insufficient if the recursive step never reaches it. Python limits call depth; a nonterminating recurrence usually raises `RecursionError`, and even a valid but deep recurrence can exceed that limit.\n\nFor non-negative integer n, factorial is n·(n−1)·…·1, with 0! = 1. This code rejects negative n. `fact(5)` calls `fact(4)`, then 3, 2 and 1. There are **5 calls total**, including the initial call, and **4 recursive child calls**. Five factorial frames are active at maximum depth. The returns are 1, 2, 6, 24 and 120. For n≥1 there are n total calls; n=0 makes one call. An iterative factorial avoids the linear call stack.';
x.vocabulary.find(v=>v.term==='Recursion').definition='Calling the same function again with a state that progresses toward a terminating case.';
x.vocabulary.find(v=>v.term==='Recursive case').definition='A step that solves the problem using recursive calls whose states make well-founded progress.';
x.vocabulary.find(v=>v.term==='RecursionError').definition='An error raised when interpreter recursion depth exceeds its limit; this can happen with missing progress or a valid very deep recursion.';
x.concepts.operations='Check the input domain, answer a base case directly, otherwise multiply n by the factorial of n−1.';
x.concepts.tradeoffs='Recursion exposes the recurrence but keeps O(n) active frames and can exceed Python\'s recursion limit.';
x.concepts.edgeCases='n=0 and n=1 return 1 immediately; negative n raises ValueError. Without the new guard, the old n<=1 test would have returned 1 for negatives, not recursed forever.';
const c=x.complexityExplanation;c.time.explanation='For n≥1 the chain ends at fact(1): n calls, with n−1 multiplications. For n=0 there is one direct call. Counting scalar multiplication as one operation gives O(n) growth.';c.space.explanation='For n≥1, n factorial frames are live on the deepest descent; n=0 uses one frame. Each frame retains O(1) scalar values under this model.';c.space.inputOutputNote='O(n) counts active frames, not bytes. n! needs Θ(n log n) output bits for growing n; arithmetic and retained integer bit lengths require a separate analysis.';c.derivation[2].description='At most max(1,n) factorial frames are active; the initial call is counted once.';c.fixedDataNote='fact(5) makes 5 calls total, 4 child calls and reaches depth 5; it returns 120. The named function excludes printing and tracer state.';c.tradeoffs='Iteration avoids recursive frames, keeping a constant number of integer variables. The growing factorial result still occupies Θ(n log n) bits.';
table(id,'O(n)','O(n)','For n≥1 there are n calls and n−1 scalar multiplications; n=0 costs O(1). Integer bit costs are excluded.');
x.prediction[0].answer='With the base-case return removed, valid n keeps decreasing until the negative-input guard raises ValueError. If both the guard and base case are removed, calls continue until RecursionError.';x.prediction[0].explanation='Termination depends on all exits in the actual program. Python\'s recursion limit is a protection, not a mathematical base case.';x.prediction[0].prompt='If the `n<=1` base-case return is removed but the negative-input guard remains, how does fact(5) stop?';
exEdit(id,'dpbc-predict-1',e=>{e.expected='5 calls (fact(5), fact(4), fact(3), fact(2), fact(1)), including the initial call once; maximum factorial-frame depth 5. There are 4 child calls.';e.hints=['List the arguments of the active calls: 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.','The initial fact(5) is already in that list.','Five calls total and depth five; four child calls.'];});
for(const e of x.exercises.filter(e=>e.tests))e.prompt+=' Assume n is a non-negative integer.';
x.references.push(ref('laws','Runestone: three laws of recursion','Base case, changing state, recursion',['A terminating recursive solution needs a base case and progress toward it.']),frames,ref('recursion','Python 3.14 recursion limit','sys.getrecursionlimit',['The interpreter limits recursion depth to protect the C stack.']));
}
{
const id='dp-recursive-calls',x=item(id);insert(id,4,'    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError("n must be non-negative")',['Check the non-negative Fibonacci-index contract.','Reject negative indices; this lesson uses F(0)=0 and F(1)=1.']);
x.explanation=x.explanation.replace(/nearly doubles[^.]*\./gi,'The call count grows by a ratio approaching φ≈1.618 for successive indices.');
x.explanation+='\n\nPrecisely, T(0)=T(1)=1 and T(n)=1+T(n−1)+T(n−2). Therefore T(n)=2F(n+1)−1, where F is the Fibonacci sequence. T(6)=25, T(7)=41 and T(8)=67. Two child calls per non-base node do not imply doubling per additional index: the children have different sizes.';
const c=x.complexityExplanation;c.time.bound='O(φ^n)';c.time.explanation='Total calls equal 2F(n+1)−1, by T(0)=T(1)=1 and T(n)=1+T(n−1)+T(n−2). This is Θ(φ^n), with φ=(1+√5)/2; O(2^n) is a looser upper bound. Successive counts approach ratio φ, not 2. fib(6) makes 25 calls.';c.derivation[1].cost='O(φ^n)';c.derivation[1].description='Two differently sized children give the Fibonacci call-count recurrence, not exact binary doubling.';c.space.explanation='Depth-first evaluation retains one path at a time. For n≥1 its longest path has n Fibonacci frames; n=0 has one. The total call tree has many more nodes than the live stack.';c.costModel='Count one call, scalar comparison, addition and counter update as constant work. A non-base call has children n−1 and n−2. The global call counter is instrumentation; integer bit costs are excluded.';c.fixedDataNote='fib(6) returns 8 and increments calls 25 times: 2F(7)−1=25. Maximum Fibonacci depth is 6. Function analysis excludes the two print calls.';
x.experiments=x.experiments.map(s=>s.replace(/[^.]*double[^.]*\.?/gi,'Reset calls to 0 before requesting fib(6), fib(7), fib(8); observe 25, 41, 67 and ratios approaching φ.'));
table(id,'O(φ^n)','O(n)','Exact total calls: 2F(n+1)−1; scalar arithmetic model; depth max(1,n).');
exEdit(id,'dprc-choose-1',e=>{e.expected='Memoization or bottom-up tabulation reuses answers to fully specified overlapping states. The cost is distinct states plus their transition work; for Fibonacci there are n+1 states and O(1) scalar work per state, giving O(n). Caching is not automatically linear for every recurrence.';e.hints=['Identify exactly which subproblem state repeats.','Cache valid answers only when the state fully describes the problem.','Count distinct states and all transition work; caching does not promise linear or polynomial time by itself.','Fibonacci has n+1 index states and constant scalar work per state, so memoization or tabulation gives O(n).'];e.recognition.modelExplanation=e.expected;e.recognition.reasons[0].text='Fully specified overlapping states can be reused; total cost includes the nonrecursive work of each state. Fibonacci has O(n) states with O(1) scalar transitions.';e.recognition.approaches[1].rejectionFeedback='A base case establishes direct answers; merely adding one does not generally eliminate repeated work. A mathematically justified alternative recurrence could change the analysis.';});
x.references.push(dp,frames);
}
{
const id='dp-backtracking',x=item(id);const c=x.complexityExplanation;
c.time.bound='O(C*n^2)';c.time.explanation='Every visited prefix can be extended to a valid length-2n string. Charging its at most 2n+1 prefix nodes to a completion gives O((n+1)C) nodes. Each immutable concatenation copies at most 2n characters, so O((n+1)^2 C) is a conservative time upper bound for this Python implementation (O(C*n^2) for n≥1). It is not asserted tight. Required output alone has Θ(nC) characters.';c.costModel='Tests and result-list append are constant/amortized work. cur + a character creates a new immutable prefix with copying work proportional to prefix length. Live ancestor frames retain their earlier strings. C is the nth Catalan number.';c.space.bound='O(n^2)';c.space.explanation='A deepest chain keeps prefixes of lengths 0,1,…,2n in separate active frames. Their total character storage is O(n²), in addition to O(n) frame overhead. Shared immutable strings need no pop, but that does not make their retained storage linear.';c.space.inputOutputNote='The result holds C strings of 2n characters plus C list references: O((n+1)C) output storage. Auxiliary immutable prefixes total O(n²) for n≥1; n=0 uses constant storage.';c.derivation=[{lines:[5,6,7],description:'Record an existing immutable string reference; append is amortized O(1), not a string copy.',cost:'O(1) per solution',dimension:'time'},{lines:[8,9,10,11],description:'At most O((n+1)C) prefix nodes, each with at most O(n+1) concatenation work; conservative bound.',cost:'O((n+1)^2*C)',dimension:'time'},{lines:[4,9,11],description:'Active frames retain prefixes of increasing length, totaling O(n²) characters.',cost:'O(n^2)',dimension:'space'}];
explain(id,'res.append(cur)','Append a reference to the completed immutable string. No string copy is performed here; later calls create different strings.');
x.explanation+='\n\nThis version passes immutable strings: each recursive call has its own longer prefix, and returning reveals the caller\'s unchanged prefix. `res.append(cur)` stores that string reference. The stack retains all ancestor prefixes, so auxiliary character storage is O(n²), although stack depth is O(n). A shared list of characters with append/pop and `"".join(path)` at completion can use O(n) working characters; it must undo mutations.';
x.concepts.tradeoffs='Pruning prevents invalid prefixes, but enumerating all valid strings still requires large output. Immutable prefixes simplify undo and retain quadratic characters across a deep chain; a shared character list can reduce working storage.';
table(id,'O(C*n^2)','O(n^2)','Conservative time upper bound for immutable concatenation, not a tight claim. C=Catalan(n); result storage O((n+1)C), excluded from auxiliary space.');
x.references.push(bt,ref('catalan','MIT: balanced parentheses and Catalan counting','Balanced-prefix condition; Catalan formula',['A valid prefix never has more closing than opening parentheses; C_n=binom(2n,n)/(n+1).'],['The app opens first, allows at most n opens, and closes only when close_c<open_c. n=0 gives one empty string.']),frames);
}
{
const id='dp-subsets',x=item(id);x.experiments=x.experiments.map(s=>s.includes('bt(i,')?'Replace bt(i + 1, path) with bt(i, path) only as a failure experiment: the same index remains eligible forever, so there is no progress and Python raises RecursionError or the app stops at a trace limit. It is not a valid reusable-choice generator without a remaining target or depth bound.':s);x.complexityExplanation.costModel='There are exactly 2^n recursive nodes and 2^n−1 child choices. Each node copies its path, whose total element count is n·2^(n−1) for n≥1. List-end append is amortized O(1); pop and reference indexing are O(1).';x.complexityExplanation.time.explanation='Every subset occurs once with increasing indices. Across all subsets, each of n elements appears in exactly 2^(n−1) copies, so output element copies are n·2^(n−1). Including 2^n list headers gives O((n+1)2^n), conventionally O(n2^n) for n≥1; empty input takes O(1).';x.complexityExplanation.tradeoffs='A count or optimum can sometimes be computed by DP without storing every subset, if a useful bounded state exists. Aggregation alone does not guarantee polynomial time. Explicitly returning all subsets still requires their output storage.';x.references.push(bt,ref('h19','Stanford CS106B handout 19','Classic exhaustive subset pattern',['Include/exclude decisions enumerate all 2^n subsets.'],['The app uses increasing-index DFS and shared append/pop rather than copied C++ strings.']),problem('subsets','LeetCode: Subsets',['Distinct input values are required for unique value subsets; every subset is returned.'],['App additionally allows empty input and chooses DFS output order.']));
}
{
const id='dp-permutations',x=item(id);const c=x.complexityExplanation;c.costModel='At each internal node this code scans all n indices, including used entries. Leaf recording copies n references. List append is amortized O(1); pop and used-array access are O(1).';c.time.explanation='There are n! complete orderings. The internal prefix nodes number Σ_{r=0}^{n−1} n!/(n−r)!, which is O(n!). Scanning n indices at each gives O(n*n!) work; n! leaf copies of length n give the same bound. It is not O(n!) branch work for this full-index-scan implementation. Empty input returns one empty ordering in O(1).';c.derivation.find(d=>d.lines.includes(8)).cost='O(n*n!)';c.derivation.find(d=>d.lines.includes(8)).description='Every internal prefix scans n indices; O(n!) prefix nodes give O(n*n!) index checks.';x.concepts.commonMistakes=x.concepts.commonMistakes.replace('permutations get truncated','later full orderings are omitted');x.experiments=x.experiments.map(s=>s.includes('used')?'Delete used[i] = False as a failure experiment: flags remain set after return, so many full orderings are lost. The full-length base case still prevents short results from being recorded.':s);exEdit(id,'dpperm-fix-1',e=>{e.prompt='For a list of distinct values, permutations(nums) should return every full ordering. The used flag is never cleared on backtrack, so later orderings are missing. Restore the flag after exploring each choice.';e.hints=e.hints.map(s=>s.replace(/truncated results|truncated permutations|truncated/gi,'missing full orderings').replace(/too short/gi,'incomplete enumeration'));});x.references.push(bt,ref('h19','Stanford CS106B handout 19','Classic exhaustive permutation pattern',['Trying every remaining unused element enumerates n! orderings.'],['Source copies strings of remaining choices; app scans a fixed n-index used array and counts that work.']),problem('permutations','LeetCode: Permutations',['Inputs are distinct; output consists of every full ordering.'],['Empty-input extension is one empty ordering.']));
}
{
const id='dp-combinations',x=item(id);x.code=x.code.replace('range(start, n + 1)','range(start, n - (k - len(path)) + 2)');explain(id,'for i in range','Try only choices that leave enough larger numbers to complete the k-item selection. range stops before n-(k-len(path))+2, so its last value is n-(k-len(path))+1.');const c=x.complexityExplanation;c.time.bound='O((k+1)*C(n,k)+1)';c.time.explanation='The capped loop visits only prefixes that extend to a size-k combination. Charge each prefix to one completion: at most (k+1)C(n,k) nodes. Each loop choice has constant amortized overhead, and each of C(n,k) output copies has length k. Thus O((k+1)C(n,k)+1). The +1 covers impossible k>n, which returns [] immediately. k=0 returns [[]].';c.space.bound='O(k+1)';c.space.explanation='One shared path and at most k+1 backtracker frames use O(k+1) auxiliary storage for 0≤k≤n; impossible k>n uses O(1).';c.space.inputOutputNote='Output contains C(n,k) list headers and k*C(n,k) element references: O((k+1)C(n,k)).';c.derivation[1].description='Capped increasing-index loop keeps every visited prefix extendable; at most (k+1)C(n,k) nodes.';c.derivation[1].cost='O((k+1)*C(n,k)+1)';c.derivation[2].cost='O(k+1)';c.assumptions.push('n and k are non-negative integers. The new remaining-slot cap is essential to this output-related time bound.');c.tradeoffs='A size check restricts subset enumeration to k items; this displayed version also prunes prefixes that lack enough remaining candidates. The minimal practice template without that cap is correct but can explore Σ_{j=0}^k C(n,j) prefixes, including all 2^n when k=n.';x.explanation+='\n\nThe displayed loop also requires enough remaining numbers to finish. If r=k−len(path) slots remain, a first choice above n−r+1 cannot work and is skipped. This cap matters for cost: without it, k=n would still visit all 2^n subset prefixes before returning the one full combination. The practice exercise shows the simpler unpruned template, whose answers are correct but whose search cost can be larger.';x.concepts.operations='Record when k items are chosen; otherwise try increasing candidates only while enough larger candidates remain to fill the missing slots.';x.experiments=x.experiments.map(s=>s.toLowerCase().includes('prun')?'Remove the remaining-slot cap by restoring range(start, n + 1); compare calls for combine(6,6): one answer in both versions, but many unnecessary prefixes in the uncapped version.':s);table(id,c.time.bound,c.space.bound,'Pruned displayed function: O((k+1)C(n,k)+1) time; practice unpruned template may explore Σ C(n,j) prefixes.');exEdit(id,'dpcomb-complete-1',e=>{e.prompt+=' This exercise uses the minimal unpruned loop; it returns correct combinations but does not inherit the displayed pruned function\'s time bound.';});x.references.push(bt,problem('combinations','LeetCode: Combinations',['Select k distinct integers from 1..n without considering ordering.'],['App extends k=0 to [[]] and k>n to []; displayed loop additionally prunes unfillable prefixes.']),problem('combination-sum','LeetCode: Combination Sum',['Distinct positive candidates may be reused; choices are limited by a remaining sum.'],['Changing only the recursion index without a target/depth bound does not establish termination.']));
}
