{
const id='dp-n-queens',x=item(id);x.code=x.code.replace('occupied "/" diagonals, keyed by (row - col)','occupied descending diagonals, keyed by (row - col)').replace('occupied "\\\\" diagonals, keyed by (row + col)','occupied ascending diagonals, keyed by (row + col)');explain(id,'diag1 =','Create the row−col conflict set: with rows increasing down and columns right, these are \\ diagonals.');explain(id,'diag2 =','Create the row+col conflict set: these are / diagonals in the displayed row/column convention.');x.explanation+='\n\nWith row numbers increasing downward and column numbers increasing rightward, **row − col** stays constant along a **\\** diagonal, while **row + col** stays constant along a **/** diagonal. The names diag1/diag2 are arbitrary; the formulas and paired removals matter. This counts all solutions rather than stopping at the first. n=0 counts one empty placement, an extension beyond the usual n≥1 problem.';const c=x.complexityExplanation;c.time.bound='O(N*N!)';c.costModel='Every nonterminal prefix scans all N columns, including attacked choices. Ignoring diagonals, distinct-column prefixes number Σ_{r=0}^N P(N,r)=O(N!). Set lookup uses expected O(1) ordinary hashing; mutation uses amortized expected O(1). Count increments are scalar operations in this model.';c.time.explanation='Distinct-column prefixes give an O(N!) upper bound on visited nodes; diagonal pruning removes more. This code still tests all N columns at each nonterminal node, so O(N*N!) is a conservative upper bound under expected hashing, not a tight claim that there are N! solutions. n=0 is constant.';c.derivation[2].cost='O(N*N!)';c.derivation[2].description='At most O(N!) column-distinct prefixes, with N candidate checks per nonterminal prefix.';c.tradeoffs='Conflict sets turn board scans into expected constant-time checks. Diagonal pruning shrinks the search, but output-sensitive counting is not inferred from the conservative bound. Symmetry can reduce exploration with additional bookkeeping.';c.fixedDataNote='n_queens(4) returns 2; it visits 17 backtracker calls and tests 60 column candidates. The O(N*N!) upper bound includes scanning attacked columns and excludes tracing/printing.';x.prediction[0].answer='For rows increasing down and columns right, row−col labels \\ diagonals and row+col labels / diagonals. The sets store occupied keys, so membership is expected O(1) with ordinary hashes.';x.prediction[0].explanation='Diagonal direction depends on coordinate convention; either invariant identifies one family. Set costs are expected, not an unconditional worst-case guarantee.';x.concepts.tradeoffs='Diagonal pruning rejects attacked squares early. Maintaining three sets gives expected O(1) conflict checks, while each nonterminal row still scans N columns.';x.concepts.edgeCases+=' n=0 gives one empty placement; n must be a non-negative integer. Larger valid searches can exceed trace/time or interpreter recursion limits.';x.bindings=[{variable:'row',model:'recursion'},{variable:'cols',model:'set'},{variable:'diag1',model:'set'},{variable:'diag2',model:'set'},{variable:'count',model:'object'}];x.experiments[0]='Try n=5 and compare its 10 solutions with n=4\'s 2. Larger boards may exceed the app\'s event/time limits; a stopped trace is not a completed count.';table(id,c.time.bound,'O(N)','Conservative upper bound includes N checks per internal node; set costs are expected; n=0 uses O(1).');exEdit(id,'dpnq-predict-1',e=>{e.expected=e.expected.replace(/'\/'.*?row - col[^.]*\./g,'row−col identifies \\ diagonals; row+col identifies / diagonals when rows increase downward.');});x.references.push(ref('h19','Stanford CS106B handout 19','The venerable 8-Queens, solve/place/remove and safety checks',['Backtracking chooses a safe square, explores the next row/column, and restores the board after return.'],['Source chooses one queen per column and stops at the first solution; app transposes to one per row, counts every solution, and stores diagonal integer keys rather than scanning a board.']),problem('n-queens-ii','LeetCode: N-Queens II',['Count nonattacking full queen placements; n=4 gives 2 and n=1 gives 1.'],['Original n≥1; app extends n=0 to one empty placement.']),bt);
}
{
const id='backtracking',x=item(id);x.whyItHelps+=' For all subsets there are no invalid subsets to prune; the output itself is exponential. O(depth) working space applies to a shared path with constant-size per-frame state. Immutable copied prefixes can retain more memory across frames.';x.conditions[3]='An increasing start index prevents duplicate index orderings for subsets; a used array prevents index reuse in permutations. Unique value outputs additionally require distinct input values or skip-equal-siblings rules.';x.alternatives[0]='DP can aggregate counts/optima when a useful bounded state exists. A feasibility/count table can also guide enumeration, but cannot replace the work of emitting every requested configuration.';x.complexityExplanation.assumptions.push('Input elements are distinct for unique value subsets. n=0 returns one empty subset.');x.references.push(bt,ref('h19','Stanford CS106B handout 19','Exhaustive permutation/subset patterns and first-solution backtracking',['Shared choice state must be restored; exhaustive generation and first-solution search have different stopping rules.']));exEdit(id,'pat-bt-recognize-1',e=>{e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='dp').label='A DP count without enumeration';e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='dp').rejectionFeedback='A count alone does not return the requested arrangements. DP can guide a later traversal, but the traversal must still emit every permutation.';e.recognition.reasons.find(r=>r.id==='overlapping-sub').text='A cached scalar count alone returns all explicit permutations without generating them.';});exEdit(id,'pat-bt-choose-1',e=>{e.prompt+=' Assume distinct non-negative integer values and a bounded non-negative target.';e.expected='(a) Backtracking emits the explicit subsets; a DP feasibility table may guide that traversal. (b) Subset-sum count DP over item index and sum can avoid enumerating each subset, with pseudo-polynomial cost in the numeric target. Neither a scalar count nor feasibility alone returns every configuration.';e.recognition.scenario=e.prompt;e.recognition.modelExplanation=e.expected;e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='dp').label='Subset-sum counts alone';e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='dp').rejectionFeedback='Counts alone answer (b), not the requested list in (a). DP can be combined with a reconstruction/enumeration traversal.';e.hints=['Listing needs each explicit configuration, not only a scalar count.','Backtracking can emit every qualifying subset.','A DP feasibility table can prune or guide a later enumeration.','With bounded non-negative sums, counting DP uses (item index,sum) states and is pseudo-polynomial in the target.'];});
}
{
const id='dynamic-programming',x=item(id);x.summary='Define reusable subproblem states and a correct recurrence; evaluate each needed state by memoization or tabulation.';x.whyItHelps='When a correct recurrence revisits the same fully specified states, reuse their answers. Top-down memoization computes states on demand; bottom-up tabulation follows a dependency order. The cost is the number of evaluated states plus all their transition work, often written states × work per state. Fibonacci has O(n) states and O(1) scalar work per state, so it becomes O(n). Other DPs may have exponential state sets or pseudo-polynomial numeric ranges: caching alone does not promise polynomial time. Optimal substructure is the optimization-specific form of a valid recurrence; counting and feasibility use their own sum/OR recurrences.';x.conditions=['A state contains every fact needed to determine its answer; repeating the same state must have the same result.','Use a correct recurrence and terminating/acyclic dependency order. For optimization, subproblem optima must compose correctly; counting/feasibility combine their appropriate answers.','Memo keys are hashable and stable for the computation. Hashable and immutable are different properties; mutable dependencies can invalidate a cache.'];x.counterexamples[2]='Caching skips repeat body execution, so side effects or fresh mutable return-object requirements can change behavior. Isolate pure computation and preserve any required effects separately.';const c=x.complexityExplanation;c.costModel='For n≥2, non-base indices 2..n are written to memo once each. Base cases 0 and 1 return directly without being cached, so they can be called more than once, a constant total here. Dict costs are expected with ordinary hashes; additions are scalar operations.';c.time.explanation='Non-base states 2..n are solved once; repeated requests reuse memo. This code leaves bases uncached: at n≥2, fib(1) returns twice and fib(0) once. Total helper calls are 2n−1, so expected-hashing scalar time is O(n), including the constant repeated base work.';c.fixedDataNote='fib(10) makes 19 helper calls and writes nine non-base memo entries. Its bases return three times in total. Naive fib(10) would make exactly 177 calls (=2F(11)−1). Function analysis excludes printing.';c.derivation[1].description='Solve and store each of the n−1 non-base states once; base cases remain uncached.';x.complexityNote='Fibonacci walkthrough: O(n) scalar time under expected hashing and O(n) cached cells/frames; n−1 non-base states cached, bases return directly. Generic DP cost depends on states and transition work.';x.references.push(dp,ref('hashable','Python 3.14 glossary','hashable',['Hashability means stable hash and compatible equality, not merely immutability.']),ref('coin','Runestone: dynamic programming','Memoization, tabulation and reconstruction',['Reuse stored answers and compute dependencies before dependents.'],['The app adopts the broader MIT/Python convention including top-down memoization as DP.']));exEdit(id,'pat-dp-choose-1',e=>{e.prompt+=' Each array position may be used once; duplicate values refer to distinct positions.';e.expected='Backtracking directly lists the configurations. DP feasibility/count results alone do not emit them, but an augmented DP table can guide reconstruction/enumeration. Listing every answer still pays for the output. To support zero or negative values without losing subsets, finish the finite include/exclude decisions and record at a leaf whose sum equals the target.';e.correctPatternId='backtracking';e.hints=['The output is a list of configurations, not only a count.','Backtracking traverses finite include/exclude decisions over array positions.','A DP table can guide reconstruction; it cannot eliminate output work.','Record qualifying leaves after all positions are decided. Returning as soon as sum reaches target can miss later zero/canceling selections.'];e.recognition.modelExplanation=e.expected;e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='dp').label='DP counts alone';e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='dp').rejectionFeedback='A scalar count alone does not return the subsets. DP augmented with a traversal can guide enumeration, but emitting the configurations remains necessary.';});
}
{
const id='knapsack',x=item(id),c=x.complexityExplanation;c.time.bound='O((n+1)*(C+1))';c.space.bound='O(C+1)';c.time.explanation='Summing input costs O(n). An odd total returns after that scan. For an even total, allocation initializes C+1 cells and each item makes at most C+1 descending checks. Including zero items/target gives O((n+1)(C+1)); for positive dimensions, commonly O(nC). C is a numeric sum, so this is pseudo-polynomial.';c.space.explanation='A C+1 boolean array retains exact reachable sums for the processed item prefix. The current item layer is implicit in the outer loop.';c.fixedDataNote='For [1,5,11,5], total22 and target11: the descending loop executes 11+7+1+7=26 updates, not exactly n*C=44. It finds 11 and 1+5+5. Function analysis excludes demo construction/printing.';c.derivation[1].cost=c.time.bound;c.derivation[2].cost=c.space.bound;x.complexityNote='O((n+1)(C+1)) scalar work, O(C+1) boolean cells; target C=total/2. Odd totals stop after O(n). Exact reachable sums use a descending 0/1 update.';x.conditions.push('Input values are non-negative integers; zeros are allowed and repeated values are distinct input positions. Negative values need a different state range/representation.');x.alternatives[1]='Fractional ratio-greedy has a guarantee when items with positive weights may be split. It has no general optimality guarantee for indivisible 0/1 items.';x.alternatives[2]='Meet-in-the-middle can help when item count is moderate and target huge: enumerate sums for two halves and search compatible pairs. Account for sum generation and sorting/search work as well as exponential half-size storage.';x.counterexamples[2]='Unlimited reuse changes the state transition. A 0/1 restriction may wrongly report impossible or give too many coins for a minimum-count objective; use an unbounded recurrence when reuse is allowed.';exEdit(id,'pat-ks-recognize-1',e=>{e.prompt+=' Assume non-negative integer values; equal values are separate item positions.';e.expected=e.expected.replace('O(n·target)','O((n+1)(target+1))');e.hints=e.hints.map(s=>s.replace('O(n·target)','O((n+1)(target+1))'));e.recognition.scenario=e.prompt;e.recognition.reasons[0].text=e.recognition.reasons[0].text.replace('O(n·target)','O((n+1)(target+1))');});exEdit(id,'pat-ks-choose-1',e=>{const fix=s=>s.replace(/would undercount coin usage/g,'may report impossible or use too many coins').replace(/would undercount/g,'may report impossible or overestimate the minimum').replace(/and undercounts/g,'and can give impossible or a nonminimal count');e.expected=fix(e.expected);e.hints=e.hints.map(fix);e.recognition.modelExplanation=fix(e.recognition.modelExplanation);e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='knapsack-01').rejectionFeedback=fix(e.recognition.approaches.find(a=>a.id==='knapsack-01').rejectionFeedback);});x.references.push(ref('mit18','MIT 6.006 lecture 18','Subset sum; take/skip states; numeric pseudo-polynomial range',['Subset-sum can use an item/target recurrence; numeric target complexity is pseudo-polynomial.'],['App compresses previous-item states using a descending sum loop; zeros and empty input are supported extensions.']),problem('partition-equal-subset-sum','LeetCode: Partition Equal Subset Sum',['Equal partition is subset-sum to half the total; each position is used at most once.'],['Original values are positive; app also supports zeros and empty input.']),ref('knap','University lecture: 0/1 knapsack','Take/skip recurrence and capacity layers',['Previous-item states enforce at-most-once use.']));
}

// The state question concerns the natural full table, not compressed storage.
exEdit('dp-1d-2d','dp12-choose-1',e=>{e.prompt="For the state 'best result using the first i items with capacity at most j', describe the natural uncompressed table and explain whether one-row storage is also possible.";e.expected='The natural state is (i,j), so a full table is dp[i][j]. One-row storage is also possible: the outer item loop supplies i implicitly, while descending j preserves previous-item states. A one-index table without that layer discipline would lose necessary state.';e.hints=['Distinguish the state coordinates from the number of stored rows.','The full table has item-prefix and capacity axes.','A descending-capacity row can preserve previous-item dependencies while the outer loop supplies the item layer.','Natural full table: dp[i][j]. Valid compression: one row with correct update direction.'];e.recognition.scenario=e.prompt;e.recognition.modelExplanation=e.expected;e.recognition.approaches[0].label='Natural full table dp[i][j], with valid row compression';e.recognition.approaches[1].label='Discard item layer with no update-order rule';e.recognition.approaches[1].rejectionFeedback='A one-row array can work when the outer loop and descending update preserve the item layer. Discarding that information without such a rule changes the recurrence.';e.recognition.reasons[0].text='The natural state has both item-prefix i and capacity j. Storage may be compressed if control flow preserves item layers and dependencies.';});
// Add input-domain notes where minimal examples intentionally rely on preconditions.
for(const id of ['dp-backtracking','dp-combinations','dp-permutations','dp-subsets']){const x=item(id);x.complexityExplanation.assumptions.push(id==='dp-backtracking'?'n is a non-negative integer; negative n is outside this generator\'s contract.':'Collection contents and scalar sizes meet the stated contract; duplicate-aware variants need additional rules.');}
// Counters record entry to a next line, not a completed assignment or a loop's body count.
for(const {x} of changes.values())for(const counter of x.complexityExplanation.counters){counter.definition=counter.definition.replace(/\(line \d+\)/g,'').replace(/line \d+/g,'the cited line').trim()+` Recorded line entries at ${counter.countLines.join(', ')} occur before the operation completes.`;}
// Existing map keys only: predictions with raw hints must never be sent as shared patches.
const sharedFile='src/content/exercise-tests-data.ts',sharedText=fs.readFileSync(sharedFile,'utf8'),tree=ts.createSourceFile(sharedFile,sharedText,ts.ScriptTarget.Latest,true),keys=new Set();
function visit(n){if(ts.isPropertyAssignment(n)&&ts.isStringLiteral(n.name)){let p=n.parent,map;while(p){if(ts.isVariableDeclaration(p)&&/^EXERCISE_(HINTS|RECOGNITION)$/.test(p.name.getText(tree)))map=p.name.getText(tree);if(ts.isCallExpression(p)&&p.expression.getText(tree)==='Object.assign'&&/^EXERCISE_(HINTS|RECOGNITION)$/.test(p.arguments[0]?.getText(tree)??''))map=p.arguments[0].getText(tree);p=p.parent;}if(map)keys.add(`${n.name.text}:${map==='EXERCISE_HINTS'?'hints':'recognition'}`);}ts.forEachChild(n,visit);}visit(tree);
const latest=[...new Map(patches.map(p=>[`${p.uid}:${p.field}`,p])).values()];
const valid=latest.filter(p=>keys.has(`${p.uid}:${p.field}`)),rawOnly=latest.filter(p=>!keys.has(`${p.uid}:${p.field}`));
for(const [id,{kind,x,before}]of changes){const codeKey=kind==='lesson'?'code':'walkthroughCode',code=x[codeKey]===before[codeKey]?undefined:x[codeKey],fields=Object.keys(x).filter(k=>!['evidence',codeKey].includes(k)&&JSON.stringify(x[k])!==JSON.stringify(before[k]));save(kind,id,x,fields,code);}
fs.writeFileSync('docs/reviews/codex-b6-shared-patches.json',JSON.stringify(valid,null,2));
fs.writeFileSync('docs/reviews/codex-b6-raw-exercise-changes.json',JSON.stringify(rawOnly.map(p=>({uid:p.uid,field:p.field,note:'Saved directly in the owned lesson/pattern; no corresponding shared-map key exists.'})),null,2));
console.log(`Saved ${changes.size} owned items. Shared patches: ${valid.length}; raw-only fields: ${rawOnly.length}.`);
